WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.257
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book four.

2
00:00:05.557 --> 00:00:09.199
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.499 --> 00:00:16.157
Unit twenty-six: Business Communication. Today you will make polite formal requests (I would appreciate it if…).

4
00:00:16.457 --> 00:00:19.086
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.033 --> 00:00:23.464
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.317 --> 00:00:25.178
Email.

7
00:00:26.183 --> 00:00:29.054
Email means a message sent electronically.

8
00:00:29.650 --> 00:00:31.905
Keep a business email short and clear.

9
00:00:32.517 --> 00:00:35.506
Your turn. Say: Email.

10
00:00:38.367 --> 00:00:39.403
Email.

11
00:00:40.150 --> 00:00:40.989
Report.

12
00:00:41.983 --> 00:00:45.817
Report means a formal document presenting information or findings.

13
00:00:46.417 --> 00:00:48.614
The quarterly report is due on Friday.

14
00:00:49.217 --> 00:00:51.892
Your turn. Say: Report.

15
00:00:54.733 --> 00:00:55.723
Report.

16
00:00:56.483 --> 00:00:57.287
Meeting.

17
00:00:58.283 --> 00:01:01.014
Meeting means a planned discussion between people.

18
00:01:01.617 --> 00:01:03.570
Send the agenda before the meeting.

19
00:01:04.183 --> 00:01:06.371
Your turn. Say: Meeting.

20
00:01:09.233 --> 00:01:09.956
Meeting.

21
00:01:10.717 --> 00:01:11.811
Concise.

22
00:01:12.817 --> 00:01:15.524
Concise means expressing much in few words.

23
00:01:16.117 --> 00:01:18.720
A concise summary saves the reader's time.

24
00:01:19.317 --> 00:01:21.865
Your turn. Say: Concise.

25
00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:25.840
Concise.

26
00:01:26.583 --> 00:01:27.341
Tone.

27
00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:31.545
Tone means the attitude expressed in writing or speech.

28
00:01:32.150 --> 00:01:34.068
Her tone was firm but polite.

29
00:01:34.683 --> 00:01:36.755
Your turn. Say: Tone.

30
00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:40.520
Tone.

31
00:01:41.283 --> 00:01:42.296
Audience.

32
00:01:43.283 --> 00:01:46.444
Audience means the people who will read or hear a message.

33
00:01:47.050 --> 00:01:49.293
Adapt your language to your audience.

34
00:01:49.883 --> 00:01:52.327
Your turn. Say: Audience.

35
00:01:55.167 --> 00:01:56.121
Audience.

36
00:01:56.883 --> 00:01:57.792
Feedback.

37
00:01:58.783 --> 00:02:01.596
Feedback means comments on how well something was done.

38
00:02:02.183 --> 00:02:05.019
Constructive feedback helped him improve the proposal.

39
00:02:05.617 --> 00:02:07.967
Your turn. Say: Feedback.

40
00:02:10.833 --> 00:02:11.811
Feedback.

41
00:02:12.550 --> 00:02:13.632
Professional.

42
00:02:14.617 --> 00:02:18.079
Professional means showing the skills and manners expected at work.

43
00:02:18.683 --> 00:02:21.960
A professional signature includes your title and phone number.

44
00:02:22.550 --> 00:02:25.121
Your turn. Say: Professional.

45
00:02:27.967 --> 00:02:28.933
Professional.

46
00:02:29.733 --> 00:02:33.152
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:34.017 --> 00:02:39.479
Visal, a supplier has delivered the wrong fabric twice. Should I write 'Send the right order now'?

48
00:02:40.583 --> 00:02:49.322
That tone is too blunt. Try: 'I would appreciate it if you could replace the order by Friday.' It's firm but professional.

49
00:02:50.417 --> 00:02:53.382
Isn't that too soft? We've lost a week.

50
00:02:54.483 --> 00:03:04.032
Be clear about consequences, politely: 'We would be grateful if you could confirm a delivery date, as further delays will affect our customers.'

51
00:03:05.117 --> 00:03:07.256
Should I mention that it's the second time?

52
00:03:08.350 --> 00:03:15.742
Yes, factually, without blame. And end constructively: 'I look forward to resolving this quickly.'

53
00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:18.284
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:19.150 --> 00:03:22.636
Grammar focus: Formal requests (I would appreciate it if).

55
00:03:22.936 --> 00:03:24.122
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:24.817 --> 00:03:27.513
I would appreciate it if you could reply by Friday.

57
00:03:28.417 --> 00:03:31.368
We would be grateful if you could confirm the delivery date.

58
00:03:32.283 --> 00:03:35.142
Would it be possible for you to send the report today?

59
00:03:36.100 --> 00:03:44.186
Don't forget 'it': I would appreciate IT if…. Other formal requests: Could you possibly…? Would you mind + -ing? I was wondering if you could…

60
00:03:44.950 --> 00:03:48.520
Be careful! Don't say: I would appreciate if you reply.

61
00:03:50.467 --> 00:03:53.848
Say: I would appreciate it if you could reply.

62
00:03:54.148 --> 00:03:55.671
Appreciate it if…

63
00:03:56.467 --> 00:03:58.922
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:03:59.733 --> 00:04:01.489
Clear, Concise, Courteous.

65
00:04:01.789 --> 00:04:07.144
Effective business communication delivers the right message to the right audience in the most efficient way.

66
00:04:08.133 --> 00:04:14.823
Readers often decide within seconds whether an email deserves attention, so the purpose should appear in the first two lines.

67
00:04:15.833 --> 00:04:22.372
Concise writing respects the reader's time, while a polite tone preserves relationships even when the message is unwelcome.

68
00:04:23.367 --> 00:04:29.128
Formal request structures, such as 'I would appreciate it if you could…', allow writers to be firm without sounding aggressive.

69
00:04:30.133 --> 00:04:38.054
Cultural awareness also matters: directness admired in some countries may seem rude in Cambodia, where indirect politeness is valued.

70
00:04:39.167 --> 00:04:43.493
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:44.350 --> 00:04:46.721
I would appreciate blank if you could call me.

72
00:04:47.021 --> 00:04:49.722
A, that. B, it. C, this.

73
00:04:53.267 --> 00:04:54.906
The answer is B: it.

74
00:04:56.050 --> 00:04:58.630
Expressing much in few words is being blank.

75
00:04:58.930 --> 00:05:02.665
A, concise. B, professional. C, formal.

76
00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:08.316
The answer is A: concise.

77
00:05:09.450 --> 00:05:11.775
The people who will read a message are its blank.

78
00:05:12.075 --> 00:05:15.600
A, feedback. B, tone. C, audience.

79
00:05:19.167 --> 00:05:21.410
The answer is C: audience.

80
00:05:22.550 --> 00:05:24.561
Choose the most polite request.

81
00:05:24.861 --> 00:05:31.568
A, Send me the file now.. B, I would appreciate it if you could send me the file.. C, You must send the file..

82
00:05:35.133 --> 00:05:39.107
The answer is B: I would appreciate it if you could send me the file..

83
00:05:40.250 --> 00:05:43.399
According to the reading, the purpose of an email should appear…

84
00:05:43.699 --> 00:05:48.583
A, at the end. B, in the first two lines. C, in the subject only.

85
00:05:52.133 --> 00:05:54.841
The answer is B: in the first two lines.

86
00:05:56.033 --> 00:05:58.999
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:05:59.850 --> 00:06:02.233
What makes a business email effective?

88
00:06:19.017 --> 00:06:20.401
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:20.701 --> 00:06:54.522
An effective business email is clear, concise and polite. The subject line should state the purpose, and the first sentence should explain exactly what the writer needs. Long background stories make readers lose patience, so it is better to use short paragraphs or bullet points. The tone matters too: a request such as 'I would appreciate it if you could reply by Friday' is far more persuasive than a demand. Finally, a professional email ends with a clear next step and a polite closing.

90
00:06:54.822 --> 00:06:56.485
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:57.383 --> 00:07:02.857
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and formal requests (i would appreciate it if).

92
00:07:03.157 --> 00:07:08.642
Today's value is professionalism. Be prepared, polite and reliable in everything you do.

93
00:07:08.942 --> 00:07:11.780
See you in Unit twenty-seven. Goodbye!

94
00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:14.465
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

